<http://localhost:5002/goe-doi-10-25625-z8xbgb> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#Dataset>.
<http://localhost:5002/goe-doi-10-25625-z8xbgb> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://doi.org/10.25625%2FZ8XBGB>.
<http://localhost:5002/goe-doi-10-25625-z8xbgb> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> "Interactive effects of tree diversity, tree identity and management on generalist predator taxonomic and functional diversity".
<http://localhost:5002/goe-doi-10-25625-z8xbgb> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "data linked to our accepted paper in Forest Ecology and Management titled: \\'Interactive effects of tree diversity, tree identity and management on generalist predator taxonomic and functional diversity\\'. Data includes raw data (spider IDs and abundances from different traps/periods), and processed data, including spider traits used to calculate functional diversity and plot-level data used for analysis with linear models. Each of these files are in seperate excel files with a metadata sheet. Spider data was collected with the use of pitfall traps from March 22 to September 6, 2022 at the BIOTREE-SPECIES biodiversity-ecosystem functioning experiment near Kaltenborn in Thuringia, Germany. The tree species pool includes European beech, Norway spruce, sessile oak, and Douglas fir planted in plots with a species richness of 1-4. Additionally, at each plot half of the area was left unmanaged and half was managed by removing 30% of the basal area and leaving the deadwood in the plot.".
