<http://localhost:5002/goe-doi-10-25625-poyd7i> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> "Replication data for: currently available medical and cosmetic cold atmospheric plasma devices affect in vivo human skin barrier and microcirculation in the context of transdermal drug delivery.".
<http://localhost:5002/goe-doi-10-25625-poyd7i> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "Four commercially available CAP devices (two medical, two cosmetic), based on dielectric barrier discharge (DBD) concepts (volume and surface discharge) were investigated in a controlled in vivo study on ten healthy volunteers. Skin barrier related parameters were assessed using transepidermal water loss (TEWL) and electrical impedance at 1 kHz. Skin hydration and microcirculation were employed to capture additional plasma-induced effects. From: C. Hellwig et al. 2026 Skin Research and Technology [Under Review]".
